/**
 * @defgroup configuration Configuration
 * @brief Configure compression parameters
 *
 * Use struct cmp_params to configure preprocessing, encoders, secondary
 * passes, and optional features. Pass an enum cmp_type value separately when
 * calculating buffer sizes and compressing data.
 * See @ref design "Compression Design" for parameter selection and behavior.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup helpers Buffer Sizing
 * @brief Calculate compression buffer requirements
 *
 * Calculate the required destination and working-buffer sizes before
 * allocating memory. Check each result with cmp_is_error() before using it as
 * a size.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup error_handling Error Handling
 * @brief Check results and retrieve error information
 *
 * Functions that return either a size or an error must be checked with
 * cmp_is_error() before the result is used as a size. Use the functions in
 * this group to retrieve the error code or a readable error message.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup context Compression Context
 * @brief Manage state across compression passes
 *
 * A context stores configuration and state between compression passes.
 * Initialise it before use, reset it before starting an independent sequence,
 * and deinitialise it when finished. Access it only through the public
 * functions in this group.
 *
 * A context may be reused for sequential compression passes. Passes using the
 * same context or working buffer must not run in parallel.
 * See @ref design_multi_pass "Multi-Pass Configuration" for sequence behavior.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup compression Compression
 * @brief Compress input samples
 *
 * Select the function matching the source data type. Compression functions
 * return the size of the compressed data on success or an error code on
 * failure.
 * See @ref design_preprocessing "Preprocessing Configuration" and
 * @ref design_encoder "Encoder Configuration" for how a pass transforms and
 * encodes its input.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup header Compression Header
 * @brief Work with metadata stored in compressed data
 *
 * The compressor writes headers internally. Use the functions in this group to
 * deserialise their host-independent fields and calculate source checksums.
 * See @ref format "Compression Header Format" for the binary layout.
 */
